WEBVTT

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:08.480
Welcome to the 10th quarterly report detailing the progress made by NASA's Ares projects on America's next launch vehicle.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:16.570
Last quarter, the project reported on upper stage confidence panels, friction stir welding simulations, and J2X power pack disassembly.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:23.700
Work from last quarter continued with more wind tunnel analysis and fabrication of the first stage's process simulation articles.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:29.970
A 1%...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.920
scale model of the Ares 1X vehicle was tested this quarter to gain high-fidelity data about

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.700
the aerodynamic environment affecting the vehicle separation of the first and upper stage.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:47.070
The separation event is critical as it occurs at nearly five times the speed of sound. Wind

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.430
can also affect a rocket prior to launch, so engineers are studying the effects of

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:56.530
ground-level winds on the Ares 1X as it rolls to the launch site and while it sits on the

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.980
launch pad. In addition to the work on Aries 1 and 1,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.540
vehicles, testing has begun on the aerodynamic environments affecting the Ares 5 cargo launch

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:11.450
vehicle when it launches late next decade. Engineers tested sensors this quarter that could be used as

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:16.530
part of the Ares 1 Integrated Vehicle Health Monitoring System. A scale model of a solid

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.130
rocket booster nozzle was manufactured with intentional defects, and sensors were placed

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:26.740
along those areas before firing. This helps engineers design an advanced warning system to

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.840
alert the crew in the event of malfunctions during an actual

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.010
flight. Ares tested the first stages drogue parachute in July. The 68 foot

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.770
diameter parachute was attached to a jumbo dart test vehicle that weighed 12

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.470
tons. The drogue chute is the second chute to deploy during re-entry. It

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.850
provides the initial deceleration of the booster before the three main

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.210
parachutes emerge to finish slowing the first stage prior to its landing and

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.870
recovery from the Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.280
The inert forward segment of the Ares solid rocket booster was inspected via a large X-ray

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.260
machine this quarter.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.320
Large X-ray film panels are passed through the center, or bore, of the motor segment,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.260
while a large X-ray machine focuses beams inward through the simulated propellant.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.560
After more than a year of modifying the facility and installing new world-class friction stir

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.980
welding tools, engineers at Marshall conducted the first demonstration of the robotic

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.220
friction stir weld tool this quarter. In order to test the welding of complex curves, the tool fused

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:42.030
together two dome panels, originally fabricated for the shuttle's external tank project. Once this

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:48.290
testing is complete, the team will start welding ARIES fuel tank domes. Using another friction stir

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:53.190
weld tool, engineers at Marshall are creating prototype tank barrels to test the processes

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:58.820
and procedures before instruments are installed for structural testing. A large-scale version

00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.960
of Marshall's Robotic Well-

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.660
tool is now being delivered and installed at the Michou Assembly Facility, where the

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.500
final flight hardware for the Ares upper stage will be created. Components are now being

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:15.250
manufactured and tested for use in the Ares-1 roll control system. Valves to control everything

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.950
from hydrazine fuel to helium are being built along with the necessary electronics to drive

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:26.080
the system automatically or by crew input. Ares roll control requires split-second timing

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.820
with high fluid pressures, so engineers at Moog have been testing.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.600
new elastomers, and improved control electronics to meet the project's needs.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:40.190
The Ares team has also been conducting assembly and handling tests for the Ares 1X test vehicle

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:45.150
roll control system. A cold flow testing unit will be used to verify that the system will

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:51.020
function properly during liftoff. Ares engineers tested a small solid rocket for use as an

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.120
ullage settling motor. This motor will be used to settle any fuel in the upper stage tanks when

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.670
the spacecraft is in orbit. Further tests are planned in the series.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.460
to help finalize the Ullage Motors design.

00:04:06.268 --> 00:04:12.268
Components from the J2X PowerPak tests article were recently removed from the stand at Stennis Space Center.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:18.880
Those components are now being disassembled and examined by the engineers to determine what effects the test sequence had on the parts.

00:04:19.356 --> 00:04:25.596
More power pack testing will be conducted in 2010 using developmental J2X turbo machinery.

00:04:26.396 --> 00:04:29.996
The J2X team continued testing a workhorse gas generator.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.240
at Marshall this quarter.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:39.300
The Workhorse Gas Generator Program conducted 53 individual tests in a variety of injector configurations.

00:04:39.596 --> 00:04:41.936
in addition to their work on the upper stage.

00:04:42.092 --> 00:04:47.132
Moog is providing actuators to control several critical valves on the J2X engine.

00:04:47.596 --> 00:04:51.836
Join us again next quarter for further developments on the first stage in our test motor.

00:04:52.108 --> 00:04:57.308
J2X gas generator testing and the fabrication of upper stage test components at Marshall.

00:04:57.676 --> 00:04:59.916
If you would like more information about Aries,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
plans and progress, please visit us on the web at nasa.gov slash ARIES.
